{"guide":{"questionsToConsider":["Which kinds of partnership do you actually want right now, and which should you remove?","What's the one fact that makes each kind of partner worth a call?","Who reads these proposals, and how quickly can they reply to the urgent ones?","Is there a minimum size or market you won't consider, and should the form say so?"],"howToUseResponses":"Group proposals by partnership type, since each one goes to a different person or needs a different yardstick. Answer the fast-timeline proposals first. For the rest, the qualifying answer does most of the sorting: an integration nobody's customers would use, or a co-marketing pitch with a tiny audience, can get a polite no. Keep the declined ones in the export, because a partner who wasn't ready this year may be next year.","customizeSteps":["Remove the partnership types you don't offer, along with their follow-up questions.","Change the reply times in both endings to what your team can really keep to.","Link to the form from your partners page or footer instead of publishing a partnerships email address."],"faqs":[{"q":"What should a partnership inquiry form ask?","a":"Who the company is, what kind of partnership they want, the one detail that shows it could work, what success looks like and their timeline."},{"q":"How do I filter out low-quality partnership pitches?","a":"Ask each type of partner a qualifying question, such as audience size for co-marketing or target market for resellers. Weak pitches usually can't answer it."},{"q":"Why do different partners see different questions?","a":"An integration partner and a reseller need different things from you. Branching asks each only what matters for their kind of deal."},{"q":"Where should I put a partnership form?","a":"On a partners or work-with-us page, linked from your footer. You can embed it directly or share it as a link."}]},"doc":{"schemaVersion":9,"title":"Partnership inquiry","description":"Hear what a would-be partner offers and wh